<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  SignPath artifact configuration for the Qualia2FA release artifacts.

  It describes what is submitted for signing, not how it is signed: certificate,
  policy and approval live in the SignPath project. The copy here is for review;
  the uploaded one is what takes effect.

  Schema: http://signpath.io/artifact-configuration/v1

  What arrives: the release job zips the tag's two installers, so a `<zip-file>`
  root carries both in one request. The version is a wildcard because
  user-defined parameters are not enabled for this project; what pins the version
  is the job, which builds the zip from the tag it is releasing.

  What is signed, and only this:

  - the Inno Setup installer (`Qualia2FA-v<version>.exe`), a Portable Executable;
  - the MSI package, and the launcher inside it, whose path comes from an
    artifact sample of a real installer: `Qualia2FA\Qualia2FA.exe`. The launcher
    is what runs after installation, so it is the signature a user's machine
    sees when the application starts.

  What is deliberately not signed: everything the sample generator listed under
  `Qualia2FA\runtime` and `Qualia2FA\app`. Those are the bundled JDK runtime
  (`jvm.dll`, `awt.dll`, the `api-ms-win-*` shims) and third-party libraries
  (`skiko-windows-x64.dll`, `jnidispatch.dll`, and the jars of Kotlin, Compose,
  coroutines, jna and skiko). They belong to their vendors, and a signature is a
  claim about who produced a file; `<jar-sign/>` would additionally rewrite jars
  this project did not build. A generated configuration that lists them is a
  description of what is present, not a decision about what to sign.

  Current state: deep signing is limited to the one file above, and no element
  here matches a file that may be absent, because an element that matches
  nothing is accepted silently.
-->
<artifact-configuration xmlns="http://signpath.io/artifact-configuration/v1">
  <zip-file>
    <pe-file
      path="Qualia2FA-v*.exe"
      product-name="Qualia2FA"
      company-name="Qualia2FA"
      copyright="Copyright (c) Qualia2FA contributors">
      <authenticode-sign />
    </pe-file>

    <msi-file path="Qualia2FA-v*.msi">
      <pe-file path="Qualia2FA\Qualia2FA.exe">
        <authenticode-sign />
      </pe-file>
      <authenticode-sign />
    </msi-file>
  </zip-file>
</artifact-configuration>